# Elephant Goldfish Model

+elephant +goldfish +model +ai +llm +cursor +harness

## TLDR

Feed the elephant: the context-rich session, and the design document it helps
write. It holds institutional knowledge, micro-decisions, the argument with the
model, and the whole history of the feature.

Test that document against the goldfish: a brand-new session with zero memory,
which knows only what is put right in front of it.

Two workflows use the same idea:

1. Research a topic. Prepare the question and the acceptance criteria with the
elephant, then have a goldfish do the research.

2. Design and implement a feature. Grow and teach the elephant until the design
document is solid, then test that document on fresh goldfish sessions before
writing code.

## Research

### 1. Victory loves preparation — Elephant

The model should act as an interrogator rather than a researcher.

I want to buy a car. It will cost me a lot of money. I have to be informed
before I make the decision. I am clearly missing critical aspects. Here is what
I know so far, and your role is to keep asking me clarifying questions until I
tell you to stop. Our job is to explore all of the important edges and
decisions. Continue to question my assumptions and force me to clarify
important points until I tell you we are done. Here is what I have come up with
so far: <assumptions go here>

Ask the model "What do you think?", and argue with it when you disagree. Argue
to learn, not to win.

If the model falls into sycophancy, break it by saying that it is not helpful,
and that it should challenge your thinking and force you to think about the
edges of the problem.

A thorough description is now ready, but it is only one side of the problem.

### 2. Trust but verify — Elephant

The description is ready. Give it to a researcher you have never worked with
before. The point is to know that the report can be trusted after it has been
produced.

Adopt the persona of an expert, skeptical fact-checker. Discuss everything that
should be in the report to give confidence that the work was thorough and
correct. Do the research once, and keep the chance to ask follow-up questions.
Say when the acceptance criteria are done.

After this step, both the question and the evaluation criteria are ready for
the final step.

### 3. Profit — Goldfish

Open a new session and put the LLM in a deep research mode.

> I have a topic I need you to think deeply about. Use any tools at your
disposal to answer this question thoroughly. I am giving you both the research
topic and the specific criteria to evaluate the quality of your final report.
Your goal is to produce a final output that thoroughly answers the question and
easily passes all the evaluation criteria.

> Here is the description of the topic I want you to research: <text from step 1>.

> Here are the criteria by which I will evaluate the quality of your output: <text from step 2>.

Let the model run its deep research for as long as it takes. The output should
be much better than a one-shot answer from a single prompt.

### 4. Judgment

Do not outsource judgment to the machine.

Save the full description, the acceptance criteria, and the final output
somewhere easy to find. Do not store the full discussion. It is mostly noise.

## Design and implementation

### Phase 1: Growing the Elephant

1. Context loading. Feed it code, docs, and anything else that already exists.
2. No-code rule. Do not write any code.
3. The sycophant challenge. Ask "What do you think?"
4. The first-draft technical proposal.
5. Ask clarifying questions until the draft is good enough.

### Phase 2: Teaching the Elephant

1. Create an absolute source of truth, and the guardrails for the code.
2. Do not ask the model to write everything at once. Build it iteratively.
3. Have it state the problem in 3–5 sentences of plain English. A casual reader
should understand the business problem.
4. Have it write a jargon-light description of the big components and how they
fit together. Include block diagrams of the major components. The goal is
explanatory prose.
5. Document alternative ideas, including the rejected ones. These become
guardrails for the code.
6. Detailed implementation. This is the longest section: a highly detailed
implementation plan. Demand an enumeration of every file that will be created
or changed, with a rationale for each. This keeps the model on the rails during
implementation.
7. Save the file. This is the first artifact.

### Phase 3: The Goldfish protocol

Start a new session for each check. When a critique is substantial, fix the
document and run that check again.

1. Comprehension test.

> Read this document and the files it references. Tell me what it's trying to
accomplish, and how my system currently works as it relates to this feature.

If the goldfish cannot explain the system from that document and the files it
references, add the missing details. Do not skip this loop.

2. Critic review.

> Assume the role of an expert technical reviewer. Read this design doc and all
the files it references. Tell me all the things I missed, all the faulty
assumptions, all the edge cases I'm missing, and things I should have
considered but did not. Every mistake and ambiguity you find makes you more
helpful and useful.

Rinse and repeat until the critiques are trivial, or disappear.

3. Implementation readiness.

> You are an experienced software engineer familiar with our codebase. Read
this document and the files it references and tell me: does it absolutely have
all the information you would require to successfully implement this feature on
your first pass?

Rinse and repeat until the critiques are trivial, or disappear.

### Phase 4: Implementation

1. Coding and guardrails.

> Read this design doc and the files it references. Implement the feature as
described. Follow the plan exactly.

2. The mean code reviewer.

> I have a strong intuition that this code is of poor quality. Please tear it
to shreds and tell me all the ways it sucks.

Tell it to flag any place that goes 10 lines without a comment. Demand strict
readability.

Rinse and repeat until the critiques are trivial, or disappear.
